</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiuwen Cao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yingbai Hu
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Shunlei Li and Jiuwen Cao are with Machine Learning and I-health International Cooperation Base of Zhejiang Province, Artificial Intelligence Institute, Hangzhou Dianzi University, Zhejiang, 310018, China. (e-mail: <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">shunlei.li@outlook.com</span> ; <span id="id1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">jwcao@hdu.edu.cn</span>)</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Longsen Gao is with the Electrical and Computer Engineering Department, The University of New Mexico, Albuquerque, NM 87131, USA. (e-mail:<span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">lgao1@unm.edu</span>)</span></span></span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Yingbai Hu is with the School of Computation, Information and Technology, Technical University of Munich, 85748 Germany. (e-mail:<span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">yingbai.hu@tum.de</span>)</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Acquiring dexterous robotic skills from human video demonstrations remains a significant challenge, largely due to conventional reliance on low-level trajectory replication, which often fails to generalize across varying objects, spatial layouts, and manipulator configurations. To address this limitation, we introduce Graph-Fused Vision–Language–Action (GF-VLA), a unified framework that enables dual-arm robotic systems to perform task-level reasoning and execution directly from RGB-D human demonstrations. GF-VLA employs an information-theoretic approach to extract task-relevant cues, selectively highlighting critical hand–object and object–object interactions. These cues are structured into temporally ordered scene graphs, which are subsequently integrated with a language-conditioned transformer to produce hierarchical behavior trees and interpretable Cartesian motion primitives. To enhance efficiency in bimanual execution, we propose a cross-arm allocation strategy that autonomously determines gripper assignment without requiring explicit geometric modeling. We validate GF-VLA on four dual-arm block assembly benchmarks involving symbolic structure construction and spatial generalization. Empirical results demonstrate that the proposed representation achieves over 95% graph accuracy and 93% subtask segmentation, enabling the language–action planner to generate robust, interpretable task policies. When deployed on a dual-arm robot, these policies attain 94% grasp reliability, 89% placement accuracy, and 90% overall task success across stacking, letter-formation, and geometric reconfiguration tasks, evidencing strong generalization and robustness under diverse spatial and semantic variations.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Multi-arm manipulation, Vision-Language-Action models, scene graph, learning from demonstration

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Developing robotic systems capable of robust, precise manipulation in unstructured, dynamic environments remains a core challenge for embodied AI: agents must perceive complex scenes, reason about physical interactions, and execute multi-step behaviors under uncertainty. Traditional vision-based control (e.g., visual servoing) relies on fixed sensor geometries and low-level visual cues, making it fragile to noise, occlusion, and contact; moreover, limited use of complementary tactile and proprioceptive modalities constrains generalization to real-world conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models leverage large-scale multimodal pretraining to map language instructions to actions, enabling semantic reasoning and task-level flexibility beyond trajectory imitation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Yet current VLAs still struggle with precise manipulation and dynamic physical interactions, lacking structured inductive biases to capture fine-grained spatial–temporal relationships; as a result, they can produce physically inconsistent plans, especially in contact-rich or ambiguous scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Parallel approaches, such as reinforcement learning, imitation learning, and transfer learning, have shown potential but remain sample-inefficient, computationally demanding, or dependent on extensive domain-specific data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Information-theoretic methods offer principled tools for reasoning about uncertainty and relevance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, yet their integration with high-level task planning has been limited. Consequently, no existing paradigm fully unifies structured physical interaction modeling with semantic task reasoning, which is essential for flexible and trustworthy robotic manipulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge this gap, we propose Graph-Fused VLA (GF-VLA), a framework that integrates structured scene graph representations with VLA reasoning as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Our method constructs temporally ordered, interaction-aware graphs from multimodal demonstrations using information-theoretic cues, and fuses them with a language-conditioned planner for semantically grounded task policies. Furthermore, we incorporate Chain-of-Thought prompting and self-verification to provide explicit subgoal decomposition and interpretable execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce an information-theoretic approach for constructing structured scene graphs from multimodal human demonstrations, explicitly encoding dynamic physical interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose GF-VLA, the first unified framework integrating structured interaction modeling with VLA reasoning, enabling robust and generalizable manipulation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We enhance interpretability by embedding Chain-of-Thought prompting into VLA models, providing transparent subgoal decomposition and improving execution reliability.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs</span></h2>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07957v1/VLA.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/198;" width="452" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An overview of the GF-VLA framework that performs policy transfer from a single human demonstration to a dual-arm robot manipulation task. <span id="S2.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">More detail: <a href="http://tiny.cc/iros25_vla" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">http://tiny.cc/iros25_vla</a></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective task representation hinges on isolating the scene’s active portion—regions exhibiting significant dynamical variation—thereby yielding compact, task-relevant abstractions while excluding inert tabletop elements. Information theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> offers a principled basis by quantifying signal information content independently of semantics; we measure activity via entropy, which captures the average uncertainty (unpredictability) of a signal. For a random variable <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> over the discrete support <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> within a temporal window <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> with mass function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}(p)=-\epsilon\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{x}}}p(x_{i})\cdot\ln{p(x_{i})},\ \epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></munderover><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}(p)=-\epsilon\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{x}}}p(x_{i})\cdot\ln{p(x_{i})},\ \epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}{:=}\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N_{\mathrm{x}}}\}=\{x{\scriptstyle(t_{\phi/2}-\tfrac{\phi}{2})},\dots,x{\scriptstyle(t_{\phi/2}+\tfrac{\phi}{2})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mfrac><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mfrac><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}{:=}\{x_{1},\dots,x_{N_{\mathrm{x}}}\}=\{x{\scriptstyle(t_{\phi/2}-\tfrac{\phi}{2})},\dots,x{\scriptstyle(t_{\phi/2}+\tfrac{\phi}{2})}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a sliding temporal window that we take to calculate entropy value <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the time step within the temporal sliding window <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete element from set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}:=x_{i}\sim\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}:=x_{i}\sim\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a random variable extracted from the set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> element in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant value. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t_{\phi/2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t_{\phi/2})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resulting entropy value, which is centered at the sliding window <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that Shannon’s entropy is measured in bits. The higher the entropy value, the more bits are required to transmit the information contained in the signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Within the proposed framework, entropy analysis is primarily applied to positional signals to characterize scene dynamics effectively. Rather than computing entropy for the entire signal simultaneously, the approach involves employing a sliding temporal window <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, entropy is calculated incrementally as this window traverses the signal over time. At each incremental shift of the temporal window, we use (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II-A Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to generate a temporal sequence of entropy <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By evaluating patterns within this entropy-derived time series, it becomes possible to discern critical fluctuations in scene dynamics, thus identifying how and when significant changes occur throughout the task execution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2509.07957v1/sub_1.png" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/109;" width="471" height="109" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((a))</span> </span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07957v1/sub_2.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/108;" width="471" height="108"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((b))</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The relocation of a single object being manipulated over time. (a). The trajectory of a single object is shown, with the sliding window <math id="S2.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the signal <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> representing the object’s position over time. (b) The entropy <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by sliding the window across <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluating the entropy of the distribution of positions within each windowed segment. A bell-shaped curve emerges, highlighting periods of significant positional change.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate the positional distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> in each temporal window <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> with a histogram: positions are quantized into fixed-width intervals <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, bin heights count occurrences, and—because <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed—the number of bins adapts to the observed positional range. Computing the associated entropy <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> exposes motion changes: uniform-speed motion yields a stable entropy profile, while deceleration increases predictability and lowers entropy. The quantization level <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> differentiates localized from extensive movement—constrained trajectories produce few high-probability bins (low entropy), whereas broad excursions populate many bins, approaching a near-uniform distribution (high entropy). Selecting <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> large enough to span repeated cycles aggregates many identical samples into the same bin (sustained low entropy), but transient, nonrepetitive segments widen the distribution and create entropy peaks; e.g., stirring a liquid maintains low entropy, whereas subsequently placing the spoon several centimeters away induces a pronounced, short-lived rise. This purely statistical approach avoids noisy velocity/acceleration estimates from numerical differentiation. As in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ II-A Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>, a 1D position trace <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated with a sliding window of width <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> yields a typically unimodal, bell-shaped entropy profile <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>; its peak at <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\phi/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\phi/2}</annotation></semantics></math> marks the event’s temporal center, while amplitude and duration reflect movement magnitude and speed. The derivative <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t</annotation></semantics></math> further delineates dynamics: <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> signals initiation, and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}(t)}/\partial t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates convergence to a new equilibrium.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A comprehensive analysis of manual tasks requires shifting from isolated scene elements to their interactions—reciprocal influences that imply information exchange. Shannon information theory offers a principled lens that we quantify shared information via mutual information <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}</annotation></semantics></math>, which measures the statistical dependence between random variables <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conceptually, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the overlap of their uncertainties between the intersection of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}+\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{Y}}-\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X}}+\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{Y}}-\mathcal{H}^{\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint entropy</em> and represents the union of the two sets:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{x}}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathrm{y}}}p(x_{i},y_{j})\cdot\ln{p(x_{i},y_{j})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></munderover><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ln</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathrm{x}}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathrm{y}}}p(x_{i},y_{j})\cdot\ln{p(x_{i},y_{j})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are discrete measurements from <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Y}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕐</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Y}_{\phi\in N_{\mathrm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{i},y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{i},y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is the joint probability of both events occurring simultaneously in the time window <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II-A Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we can conclude that if <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa, due to the symmetry of this measure. In other words, knowledge about the value of one variable provides no information about the other variable. Since we are interested in observing the dynamics of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}</annotation></semantics></math> over time, we apply the same approach described earlier, shifting the time window <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> along the considered signals to obtain <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}(t)|\mathcal{Y}(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}(t)|\mathcal{Y}(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Applying mutual information <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}</annotation></semantics></math> to position signals quantifies kinematic dependence between entities, enabling robust detection of hand–object coupling in manual tasks. Unlike velocity-based correlations, <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}</annotation></semantics></math> captures both linear and nonlinear dependencies; for example, during transport of multiple items on a tray, it correctly treats the hand and payload as a single kinematic unit despite minor sliding or oscillations. In a canonical grasp-and-move sequence as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ II-A Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>, the time-varying <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{\mathcal{X}|\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> between object <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and hand <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, computed via a sliding window, exhibits a distinct unimodal peak confined to the coordinated-movement interval and remains near zero during pre-grasp approach and post-release retreat—disambiguating genuine interaction from incidental proximity in cluttered scenes. For 3D analysis, we aggregate across axes: for hand <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and object <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}\in\mathbb{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}\in\mathbb{O}</annotation></semantics></math> (with cardinalities <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h},N_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h},N_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>), the total mutual information is</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}=\sum_{a\in\{x,y,z\}}\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}^{(a)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}=\sum_{a\in\{x,y,z\}}\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}^{(a)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07957v1/Explain_Figure/ho_oo_overview.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/178;" width="476" height="178" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) The conceptual representation of the dual-hand selection policy. The framework depends on the priority of the Left hand <math id="S2.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> or the Right hand <math id="S2.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is optimal for interacting with the manipulated object <math id="S2.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to move it to the target pose. (b) denotes <span id="S2.F3.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Coupled-Motion</span> integration between only the left-hand <math id="S2.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and one manipulated Jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) denotes the <span id="S2.F3.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span> interaction between <math id="S2.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and one Jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. (d) denotes <span id="S2.F3.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> interaction between the manipulated jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and one background jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> on the table. (e) denotes the <span id="S2.F3.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T-OO</span> interaction between one manipulated jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and current background jenga block <math id="S2.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> when the hand is shaken to make the building blocks shift slightly near the target position.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scene Graph Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a scene graph, which is a directed graph data structure, to represent the system state and the relationships between its components. Formally, a graph can be denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{SR}:=(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">SR</mtext><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{SR}:=(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}:=(\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}},\mathbb{R}_{\in N_{\mathrm{r}}},\mathbb{E}_{\in N_{\mathrm{e}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}:=(\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}},\mathbb{R}_{\in N_{\mathrm{r}}},\mathbb{E}_{\in N_{\mathrm{e}}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a tuple in which <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> number nodes, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of relationships between the nodes with number <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of edges with number <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=f\sim\mathbb{K}_{\phi\in N_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝕂</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=f\sim\mathbb{K}_{\phi\in N_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a combined set of frames which we extract from the learning video during a temperoal sliding window. In our framework, the nodes <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}:=(\lambda_{i},\Lambda_{i})\in\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}:=(\lambda_{i},\Lambda_{i})\in\mathbb{V}_{\in N_{\mathrm{v}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of attributes and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is its class identity, correspond to the entities within the scene, specifically hands and objects. For our application, the sole attribute is the entity’s 6D pose <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. The directed edges <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\in\mathbb{E}_{\in N_{\mathrm{e}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\in\mathbb{E}_{\in N_{\mathrm{e}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the interactions between these entities. Within the context of uni-manual tasks, interactions are classified into two principal categories:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">H<span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">and-</span>O<span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">bject (HO) Interactions</span></span>: denotes the relationship that pertains to the coupling between a hand and a manipulated object.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">O<span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">bject-</span>O<span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">bject (OO) Interactions</span></span>: denotes the relationship between one moving manipulated object in hand and another static object in the background.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interaction detection between any two entities instantiates a directed edge between their corresponding nodes in the scene graph. Generation proceeds by first identifying <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hand–object (HO)</span> interactions, reflecting the hand’s role as the primary effector of state change. <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Object–object (OO)</span> interactions are then evaluated only conditionally—i.e., if at least one <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HO</span> interaction was detected; otherwise, OO analysis is bypassed.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Hand–Object Interactions Detection</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define two classes of Hand-Object (<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">HO</span>) interactions as following:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Coupled-Motion</span>: denotes interaction by the active displacement of an object by the hand, indicating that the hand holds the object and moves simultaneously.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span>: denotes a state of relative static contact between the hand and object, wherein the hand is in physical contact with an object but no joint movement is observed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If the proximity condition is satisfied, the interaction is classified based on the mutual information, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. An active <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Coupled-Motion</span> is declared if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{ho_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{ho_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than a near-zero threshold <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\boldsymbol{\varpi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\boldsymbol{\varpi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, signifying strong kinematic coupling. Conversely, a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span> interaction is established or maintained if either the mutual information is concurrently decreasing and below the threshold (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}}{\partial t}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}}{\partial t}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}&lt;\gamma{\boldsymbol{\varpi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o_{i}}&lt;\gamma{\boldsymbol{\varpi}}</annotation></semantics></math>), or if a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span> state was already present in the previous frame <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Any established interaction is terminated once the distance <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}_{h,o_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}_{h,o_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> subsequently exceeds the threshold <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\mathrm{th}}_{h,o}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mi>th</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\mathrm{th}}_{h,o}\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By design, the state <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span> is entered only following a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Coupled<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>Motion</span> phase, preventing transient, non-manipulative contacts from being misclassified as salient interactions and thereby improving robustness in cluttered scenes. Detection then proceeds iteratively over all objects <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{k}\in\mathbb{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{k}\in\mathbb{O}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{O}|=N_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{O}|=N_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>), ordered by increasing proximity to the hand <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>; the search for hand–object coupling halts at the first positive detection, after which the pipeline advances to object–object (OO) interaction analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Object–Object Interactions Detection</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define two classes of Object-Object (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math>) interactions in static as following:</p>
<ul id="S2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Efficient OO (E-OO)</span>: denotes an interaction between a manipulated object <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and a stationary background object <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> that is identified as being stable, intentional, and integral to the execution of a task. This type of interaction is considered essential and is subsequently mapped into robot command primitives during plan generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Transitory OO (T-OO)</span>: denotes incidental interaction between a manipulated object <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and a stationary background object <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> that is identified as being unstable, transitory, and not representative of the core activity. These interactions are considered non-essential and are filtered out during the task segmentation process, meaning they are not translated into robot commands.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Static <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> interactions require spatial proximity: the mean distance <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}&lt;r^{\mathrm{th}}_{\mathrm{oo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>oo</mi><mi>th</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}&lt;r^{\mathrm{th}}_{\mathrm{oo}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When this holds, an <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> interaction is established or confirmed if (i) a <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Docked</span> relation exists between the hand <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and manipulated object <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, or (ii) <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> was present at frame <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, post-manipulation holding of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is correctly treated as a salient static relation. During <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Coupled-Motion</span> (active manipulation), mutual information is ill-suited when a stationary object has near-zero positional entropy, so we instead use the entropy of the mean distance, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math>: the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> interaction is labeled <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">efficient</em> if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})/\partial t&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})/\partial t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (converging to a stable configuration) and <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Transitory</em> otherwise; the <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> label persists unless <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> later exceeds <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\mathrm{th}}_{\mathrm{oo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>oo</mi><mi>th</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\mathrm{th}}_{\mathrm{oo}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, moving <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> past <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a rise in distance entropy after initial proximity, yielding a <em id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Transitory</em> label before termination by the distance threshold. Evaluation iterates over candidate background objects <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, ordered by increasing distance from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and stops at the first detected <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> relation; requiring all static <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> edges to involve <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> prevents spurious links among incidental tabletop objects and stabilizes graph topology in clutter. After integration, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{SR}[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{SR}[k]</annotation></semantics></math> matches one of the topologies in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ II-A Information Theory in Robotic Manipulation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. If both a hand–object (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HO</annotation></semantics></math>) and a subsequent <span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RS-OO</span> relation are detected, the graph at time <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is fully specified by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{t}=\{h,o_{1},o_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{t}=\{h,o_{1},o_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=(\mathcal{L}_{h},\mathbf{p}_{h}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=(\mathcal{L}_{h},\mathbf{p}_{h}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{1}=(\mathcal{L}_{o_{1}},\mathbf{p}_{o_{1}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{1}=(\mathcal{L}_{o_{1}},\mathbf{p}_{o_{1}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{2}=(\mathcal{L}_{o_{2}},\mathbf{p}_{o_{2}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{2}=(\mathcal{L}_{o_{2}},\mathbf{p}_{o_{2}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>; directed edges <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{t}=\{e_{h\rightarrow o_{1}},e_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{t}=\{e_{h\rightarrow o_{1}},e_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; and relations <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{t}=\{r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}},r_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{t}=\{r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}},r_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is annotated with its type and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi_{h,o_{1}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi_{h,o_{1}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{o_{1}\rightarrow o_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> with its static type. If no static <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> relation is found, the graph reduces to the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HO</annotation></semantics></math> pair: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}=\{h,o_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}=\{h,o_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}=\{e_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}=\{e_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}=\{r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}=\{r_{h\rightarrow o_{1}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>3 </span>Dynamic Learning Policy of Dual-Hand Selection</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Dual‑arm execution requires allocating the grasping role to one of two hands so as to (i) reduce cumulative reach–grasp–place cost and (ii) mitigate inter‑arm interference. We formulate a dynamic, learning‑augmented selector that is kinematically grounded by a contralateral prior and refined from demonstrations. For the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>‑th placement subtask with target pose <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (near the workspace midline) and source stack <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (left/right piles), we define the hand‑selection state at time <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\triangleq\big\{r_{h_{L},d_{i}},\,r_{h_{R},d_{i}},\,r_{h_{L},o_{i}},\,r_{h_{R},o_{i}}\big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\triangleq\big\{r_{h_{L},d_{i}},\,r_{h_{R},d_{i}},\,r_{h_{L},o_{i}},\,r_{h_{R},o_{i}}\big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{h,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{h,p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\big\|p-p_{h}(t)\big\|_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\big\|p-p_{h}(t)\big\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L},h_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L},h_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the left/right end‑effectors with Cartesian positions <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{h}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{h}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. The action space is</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{\textsc{use‑left‑hand},\,\textsc{use‑right‑hand}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">use‑left‑hand</mtext><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">use‑right‑hand</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{\textsc{use‑left‑hand},\,\textsc{use‑right‑hand}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a deterministic prior policy that embodies the crossing‑arm strategy: grasp with the hand contralateral to the target placement side. Equivalently, choose the hand opposite the one closer to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}(s_{t})=\begin{cases}\textsc{use‑left‑hand},&amp;r_{h_{R},o_{i}}&lt;r_{h_{L},o_{i}},\\[2.0pt]
\textsc{use‑right‑hand},&amp;r_{h_{L},o_{i}}\leq r_{h_{R},o_{i}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">use‑left‑hand</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">use‑right‑hand</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}(s_{t})=\begin{cases}\textsc{use‑left‑hand},&amp;r_{h_{R},o_{i}}&lt;r_{h_{L},o_{i}},\\[2.0pt]
\textsc{use‑right‑hand},&amp;r_{h_{L},o_{i}}\leq r_{h_{R},o_{i}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This rule shortens the combined reach–grasp–place path and lowers cross‑arm interference during placement. Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{\star}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{\star}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the grasping hand used by the human demonstrator at decision point <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (extracted from the <span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HO</span> graph), and let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> be a stochastic selector as a two‑way MLP classifier that outputs a distribution over <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. We provide an imitation‑shaped scalar signal</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a_{t},a_{t}^{\star})\;=\;\begin{cases}+R_{\text{bonus}},&amp;a_{t}=a_{t}^{\star},\\
-R_{\text{penalty}},&amp;a_{t}\neq a_{t}^{\star},\end{cases}\quad R_{\text{bonus}},R_{\text{penalty}}&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>bonus</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>penalty</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>bonus</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>penalty</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a_{t},a_{t}^{\star})\;=\;\begin{cases}+R_{\text{bonus}},&amp;a_{t}=a_{t}^{\star},\\
-R_{\text{penalty}},&amp;a_{t}\neq a_{t}^{\star},\end{cases}\quad R_{\text{bonus}},R_{\text{penalty}}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the train <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> by weighted cross‑entropy (or equivalently, bandit‑style policy gradient with (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In II-B3 Dynamic Learning Policy of Dual-Hand Selection ‣ II-B Scene Graph Generation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) as advantage). Concretely, we minimize</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}(\theta)=w_{+}\,\mathbf{1}[a_{t}^{\star}]\,-\,w_{-}\,\mathbf{1}[\bar{a}_{t}^{\star}]\cdot\left(-\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}^{\star}\mid s_{t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}(\theta)=w_{+}\,\mathbf{1}[a_{t}^{\star}]\,-\,w_{-}\,\mathbf{1}[\bar{a}_{t}^{\star}]\cdot\left(-\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}^{\star}\mid s_{t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{+}=R_{\text{bonus}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>bonus</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{+}=R_{\text{bonus}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{-}=R_{\text{penalty}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>penalty</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{-}=R_{\text{penalty}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{t}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{t}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> the complement action. This yields a selector aligned with expert preferences while remaining robust to geometric variations. At run time, we fuse the contralateral prior (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II-B3 Dynamic Learning Policy of Dual-Hand Selection ‣ II-B Scene Graph Generation ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with the learned selector by biasing the logits toward the prior action:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(a\mid s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(a\mid s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s_{t})\;+\;\kappa\,\mathbf{1}\!\left[a=\pi_{0}(s_{t})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mpadded style="width:0.406em;" width="0.406em"><mi>κ</mi></mpadded><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s_{t})\;+\;\kappa\,\mathbf{1}\!\left[a=\pi_{0}(s_{t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}J(a\mid s_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}}J(a\mid s_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> controls trust in the prior (recovering the deterministic rule as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>). When an <span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E‑OO</span> Docked relation is active (the manipulated object is stably held against its background support), the current hand assignment persists to avoid mid‑task role flips; otherwise (4) is re‑evaluated at each decision point.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Graph-Fused VLA (GF-VLA)</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System architecture</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ II Information-Theoretic Scene Graphs ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a two-stage framework: (i) Policy Generation from Human Demonstrations and (ii) VLA-based Execution with Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Reasoning. In Stage 1, raw videos are transformed into information-theoretic behavior graphs and temporal keyframes that encode spatial–semantic structure; a Policy Agent reasons over these graph–frame abstractions to produce a robot-executable policy—an ordered subtask sequence with an explicit reasoning trace—under a CoT + self-verification scheme. In Stage 2, the demonstration-derived CoT policy, live RGB input, and spoken instructions are fused by our GF-VLA model to adapt the policy to the current scene. The model outputs (a) an action head for low-level commands and (b) an LLM head for hierarchical planning, subtask decomposition, and CoT-based reasoning. During execution, continuous self-verification tracks progress, detects inconsistencies, and triggers local replanning, yielding robust, interpretable, and adaptive multi-step manipulation in unstructured environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07957v1/overview_v1.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/171;" width="452" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Policy transfer from a single human demonstration to a novel dual-arm robotic assembly task. The framework processes multimodal inputs, including language commands and visual scene data, using a SAM 2-based module to extract and project features into a shared embedding space. At its core, a unified Large Language Model (LLM) employs a dual-head structure: the LLM Head performs high-level semantic planning and validation using Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and self-verification, while the Action Head generates low-level, executable manipulator actions. This integrated design enables the robot to de-tokenize abstract reasoning into physically grounded joint and gripper commands, translating high-level goals into robust, real-world execution.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unified Dual-Head Architecture for Vision-Language-Action Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GF-VLA extends the standard VLA paradigm with four components: (i) a visual module that segments RGB and encodes RGB-D with binary masks into patch-level embeddings; (ii) a lightweight two-layer MLP projector for cross-modal token alignment; (iii) a language module built on a pretrained 7B-parameter LLaMA-2 backbone adapted with the Open X-Embodiment corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, augmented for hierarchical policy generation, Chain-of-Thought (CoT) decomposition, and self-verification; and (iv) two task-specific output heads—an LLM Head for structured semantic planning and interpretable reasoning, and an Action Head for low-level motion control. Unlike prior VLA designs, GF-VLA integrates both heads within a unified transformer encoder, enabling parallel reasoning and action under a shared semantic representation. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="III-A System architecture ‣ III Graph-Fused VLA (GF-VLA) ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A</span></a>, top-down RGB-D frames and transcribed voice instructions (via a Gladia ASR module) are fused by projecting visual patches and masks through the MLP and concatenating them with prompt tokens to the language backbone. At run time, the LLM Head emits a one-shot structured reasoning trace, while the Action Head streams 5 Hz control commands—Cartesian end-effector poses and gripper states—decoded and dispatched to the corresponding robotic arms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chain-of-Thought Guided Semantic Policy Planning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robots acting in the open world must produce plans with logical structure, causal coherence, and commonsense alignment, yet LLM-based planners often default to “fast thinking”—single-step, reactive outputs ill-suited to multi-stage manipulation. We therefore integrate Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning into GF-VLA’s policy generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, prompting the Planning Agent to explicitly articulate intermediate reasoning, decompose high-level goals into interpretable subgoals, and slow decision-making for greater logical consistency, correctness, and auditability. Concretely, the CoT-enhanced agent yields a behavior tree aligned with human task logic whose nodes specify action type and parameters together with step-wise rationale and self-verification criteria. In constructing the letter “R,” for example, the robot first executes a dual-arm grasp (<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PickObjDual</span>) to acquire two foundational blocks for the vertical backbone, then performs a coordinated dual-arm placement (<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PlaceObjDual</span>) to form the diagonal leg; each node includes a reasoning statement and a verification clause grounded in sensor feedback (e.g., gripper status, object pose, scene stability). This interpretable, CoT-guided plan supports temporal consistency, symbolic understanding, and robust execution in complex bimanual manipulation tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parameter-Efficient Multi-Head Fine-Tuning with LoRA</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt parameter-efficient fine-tuning via LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, inserting separate adapters for the LLM and Action Heads within shared LLaMA-2 transformer blocks while freezing the visual encoder and projector; only the LLaMA backbone and LoRA adapters are updated. The LLM Head is trained with policy-level supervision from human demonstrations: 250 RGB videos by 10 participants performing letter-based symbolic assembly and tower construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Videos are segmented into graph-based keyframes and paired with expert strategies; 125 demonstrations supervise next-token prediction over plan steps and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, and 125 are held out for policy-generation evaluation (Subsec.5̃.2).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Action Head is fine-tuned on 240 bimanual trials collected on a dual-arm platform—UR5e + Robotiq 2F-85 and UR10e + Barrett BH282—using a top-mounted Intel RealSense D435i for overhead RGB-D perception. Four task classes capture core skills: (i) shape generalization across cuboid/triangular-prism/cube; (ii) spatial relations under ambiguous instructions; (iii) absolute 6D pose execution; and (iv) relative pose execution. Each class × 3 shapes × 20 reps yields <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 3\times 20=240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 3\times 20=240</annotation></semantics></math> trials, recorded as synchronized dual-arm sequences with role-specific assignments and 6-DoF end-effector targets; supervision uses regression losses on Cartesian poses and gripper states. We split 120/120 for fine-tuning/evaluation. Training alternates policy and action batches, activating the corresponding LoRA branch and loss; GF-VLA fine-tunes in 4̃0 h on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090 and attains a 7.56 s average instruction-inference latency with bfloat16, enabling interpretable reasoning and robust dual-arm manipulation across diverse spatial configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Results</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task representation and planning</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.07957v1/Fig_setup_human.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/301;" width="476" height="301" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((a))</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.07957v1/Fig_setup_robot.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/299;" width="476" height="299" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((b))</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Configuration of the experimental environment and the associated camera viewpoint during the human demonstration phase. (b) The dual-arm robot system workspace and the visual feed are captured as the robot executes the task.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07957v1/Fig_exp_final.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/220;" width="476" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Information-theoretic analysis and temporal segmentation of a human demonstration for a block assembly task. (a) Keyframes from the demonstration video showing the human assembling the letter “R”. (b) Analysis of the Hand-Object (HO) interaction, plotting the mutual information (<math id="S4.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϖ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\varpi}_{h,o}</annotation></semantics></math>) and relative distance (<math id="S4.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{r}_{h,o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{r}_{h,o}</annotation></semantics></math>) between the hand and the manipulated block to detect kinematic coupling. (c)-(d) Analysis of Object-Object (OO) interactions, plotting the entropy of the relative distance (<math id="S4.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\overline{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\overline{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}})</annotation></semantics></math>) and the distance itself (<math id="S4.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{r}_{o_{m},o_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>) between the in-hand block and other objects to identify stable placements. (e) The resulting temporal segmentation of the task classifies the demonstration into a sequence of distinct interaction primitives based on the information-theoretic metrics.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first experiment aimed to assess the functionality of the video processing module and the quality of the task representations derived from it. These representations served as input to the LLM, forming the foundation for downstream task planning and policy generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the dual-hand construction of the letter “R” as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ IV-A Task representation and planning ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the participant first chose a hand (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>) and placed <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from two visible groups. During motion, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math> briefly formed an <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="OO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">OO</annotation></semantics></math> relation with another <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the table at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=40</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}_{o,o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}_{o,o}</annotation></semantics></math> below threshold), but the rising distance-entropy <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})</annotation></semantics></math> labeled it <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T-OO</span>, indicating that explicit table encoding was unnecessary. In alternate trials with slightly different initial <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math> poses, the hand skirted a box edge (treated as an obstacle), yielding a persistent <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> that lasted until separation at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=80</annotation></semantics></math>, coincident with a topology change. Approaching the table, a further <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> between <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the workspace was detected at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=70</annotation></semantics></math> via decreasing <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})</annotation></semantics></math> (descending red curve). No scene graph was emitted during the weighing phase because the hand disengaged from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C1</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. After re-grasping <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C2</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a brief proximity-induced <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T-OO</span> with the workspace dissolved as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})</annotation></semantics></math> rose; the scene-graph configuration at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=552" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>552</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=552</annotation></semantics></math> then remained stable until <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C2</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C2}}</annotation></semantics></math> moved away. When <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{object_{C2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>object</mi><mi>C2</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{object_{C2}}</annotation></semantics></math> encountered the previously placed block, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T-OO</span> transitioned to <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">E-OO</span> as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\bar{r}_{o,o})</annotation></semantics></math> decreased (dark gray region), with overall topology unchanged from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=560" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>560</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=560</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="585" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>585</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">585</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ IV-A Task representation and planning ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> shows that the proposed method sustains high performance across task types for both Graph Representation Accuracy (GRA) and Task Segmentation Accuracy (TSA). In single-hand manipulation—characterized by low interaction complexity and well-separated actions—the system attains the highest scores (GRA 98.5%, TSA 95.6%). In letter-block assembly, despite bimanual coordination and dense layouts, GRA remains 97.2% and TSA 93.9%; errors are primarily due to temporal overlaps and ambiguous onsets of placement actions. Tower construction introduces vertical stacking, depth occlusion, and rapid execution; while GRA stays strong at 96.8%, TSA drops to 93.1%, indicating that temporal segmentation is more sensitive to rapid transitions and occlusions than spatial graph inference. Overall, Fig. <a href="#S4.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ IV-A Task representation and planning ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> indicates robust extraction of spatial and temporal structure with graceful degradation as task complexity increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ IV-A Task representation and planning ‣ IV Experimental Results ‣ Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a> further shows that the VLA model’s LLM head produces accurate plans and coherent reasoning traces. For single-hand tasks, plan coverage is 98%, ordering accuracy 95%, and verification correctness 96%, with chain-of-thought (CoT) interpretability rated 4.7/5. In letter-block assembly, coverage reaches 93% and ordering 88%; minor inversions in symmetric subtasks do not affect task validity, while CoT remains clear (4.3/5) and verification is 89%. Tower construction yields 90% coverage and 85% ordering; CoT interpretability is 4.1/5 with occasional overgeneralization, and verification is 86%. Collectively, these results demonstrate strong inference of subgoal structure, temporal dependencies, and causal rationales, while highlighting opportunities for improved fine-grained temporal modeling and object-level reasoning to further enhance generalization and robustness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07957v1/acc.svg" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/325;" width="476" height="325"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((a))</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07957v1/score.svg" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/365;" width="476" height="365"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">((b))</span> </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Comparison of Graph Representation Accuracy (GRA) and Task Segmentation Accuracy (TSA) across different task types. (b) LLM-generated plan and reasoning evaluation scores across tasks. All scores normalized to [0, 1] for visualization.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Block manipulation assessment</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table I</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of Dual-Arm Manipulation under Different Task Conditions</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:85.2pt;vertical-align:-40.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(23.4pt,-5.8pt) scale(1.15670212773577,1.15670212773577) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Task Type</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GSR</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">PSR</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">6DPE (cm, <sup id="S4.T1.5.1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_sup">∘</sup>)</span></td>
